  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Integrated Surd–Index Identity Patterns",
    "description": "Use shape recognition to collapse the chapter’s advanced surd and exponent expressions before heavy arithmetic begins.",
    "teachingApproach": "Frame the lesson as pattern spotting: name the hidden algebraic identity first, substitute a compact symbol if useful, and only then compute. The varied-looking questions stay coherent because every one is solved by replacing brute force with a structural shortcut.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 32,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 9, Surds and Indices. Approved scope: Use this as the chapter capstone: the learner already knows the rules, so the skill is recognising which shape makes a large expression collapse. Cover substitutions such as u=a^(1/4) turning (u−1)(u³+u²+u+1) into u⁴−1, cyclic exponent products whose total exponent telescopes to zero, the identity a³+b³+c³−3abc when a+b+c=0, prime-factor counting after fractional powers, the cube identity behind (x+y−z)³+27xyz when cube roots add, simple consequences of abc=1, and a final pair of exponent equations solved by adding/subtracting the resulting linear relations. Each example should explicitly name the hidden shape before doing arithmetic."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "This final lesson is about one exam skill: spotting the shape before touching the numbers.\n\nA long expression may secretly be a geometric factorisation, a telescoping exponent sum, or a cube identity. The reward for seeing the shape is that most of the expression disappears.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Suppose u=2^(1/4). Then u^4=2.\n\nNow look at (u−1)(u^3+u^2+u+1).",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "This is the geometric factorisation:\n\n(u−1)(u^3+u^2+u+1)=u^4−1\n=2−1=1\n\nThe substitution turns fractional powers into ordinary algebra.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
